🔥 14 File Download System → Sensitive Document Leak
🏗️ Architecture
User → App → File Storage (S3 or local)
Download endpoint:
GET /download?fileId=48291
Database:
files
----------------
id
owner_id
path

🔎 Vulnerable Code
SELECT path FROM files WHERE id = ?;
No ownership check.

🧨 Attack Chain
Step 1 – Attacker downloads their own file.
Step 2 – They change:
fileId=48291 → 48292
Step 3 – That file belongs to another user.
Step 4 – App fetches and streams file.
Sensitive document leaked.

💡 Why This Happens
Object identity ≠ authorization.

🛡️ Fix
SELECT path FROM files 
WHERE id = ? AND owner_id = current_user.id;
Even better:
	• Generate short-lived signed URLs only after authorization.

🔥 15. Admin Role Upgrade → Vertical Privilege Escalationon
Now we go deeper.
🏗️ Architecture
User → API → Role Table
Endpoint:
PATCH /api/users/{id}/role

🔎 Vulnerable Code
UPDATE users SET role = ? WHERE id = ?;
Backend checks:
	• User is authenticated
	• User has “manager” role
But does NOT check:
	• Can manager modify THIS user?

🧨 Attack Chain
Step 1 – Attacker is “manager”.
Step 2 – They modify:
PATCH /api/users/attackerId
{
  "role": "admin"
}
Step 3 – Backend allows because:
	• Manager role exists
	• Update query succeeds
Now attacker becomes admin.

💡 Why This Happens
Authorization is role-based only.
Not object-sensitive.
Vertical privilege escalation via IDOR.

🛡️ Fix
Authorization must enforce:
	• Only admins can change roles
	• Or cannot modify own role
	• Or cannot assign higher privilege than self
Access control must be contextual and hierarchical.

