GraphQL encourages field-level development.
Authorization logic gets scattered or forgotten.

🛡️ Fix
Each resolver must enforce:
if (order.userId != currentUser.id) deny();
Or use centralized policy engine.

🧠 The Pattern Behind All 5
Every IDOR chain follows this structure:
1. User controls object identifier
2. Backend retrieves object by ID
3. Backend fails to verify ownership or permission
4. Sensitive data exposed or modified

“What is IDOR at architecture level?”
IDOR is an object-level authorization failure where the system enforces authentication but does not verify whether the authenticated user has access rights to the specific object referenced.

-> Authorization Use Cases:
	• IDOR (Broken Object Level Authorization)
	• Horizontal privilege escalation
	• Vertical privilege escalation
	• Mass assignment
    . Missing function-level access control

1. IDOR → Unauthorized Data Access → Data Breach
Real-world scenario
A document management SaaS platform where users upload and view documents.
Architecture:
                 Internet
                    │
                    ▼
            ┌────────────────┐
            │   Web Browser  │
            │     (User)     │
            └────────────────┘
                    │
                    │ HTTPS
                    ▼
          ┌────────────────────┐
          │   Web Application  │
          │  Document Service  │
          └────────────────────┘
                    │
                    ▼
              ┌────────────┐
              │  Database  │
              │ documents  │
              └────────────┘

Normal Flow:
User requests a document.
GET /api/documents/84521
Cookie: session=abc123
Server executes:
SELECT * FROM documents
WHERE id = 84521;
Returns:
Document owner = user123
But no ownership verification happens.

Attack Flow:
Attacker changes ID.
GET /api/documents/84522
Server returns another user’s document.

Internal Vulnerability:
The authorization check is missing.
Proper logic should be:
SELECT * FROM documents
WHERE id = 84521
AND owner_id = current_user
But the system only checks:
id = 84521

Why this happens in real systems?
Developers assume:
Authentication = Authorization
But authentication only answers:
Who are you?
Authorization answers:
What are you allowed to access?
If object ownership validation is skipped → IDOR occurs.

Real attack chain:
Attacker account
       │
       ▼
Enumerate object IDs
       │
       ▼
Access sensitive objects
       │
       ▼
Download documents
       │
       ▼
Large-scale data breach

Mitigation:
Two key protections:
Object ownership verification
if document.owner_id != user.id
    deny access

Use indirect object references
Instead of:
/api/documents/84521
Use:
/api/documents/a7b92d3f
Random IDs prevent enumeration.

2. Horizontal Privilege Escalation → Account Manipulation
Horizontal escalation means:
User A accesses resources belonging to User B
Same privilege level, different accounts.

Real-world scenario:
An e-commerce platform where users can update their shipping address.

Architecture:
        Browser
           │
           ▼
     Web Application
           │
           ▼
      User Service
           │
           ▼
        Database

Normal Update Flow:
User sends request:
PUT /api/users/128/address
Body:
{
 "address": "New Delhi"
}
Server updates:
UPDATE users
SET address = "New Delhi"
WHERE id = 128

Attack Flow:
Attacker changes user ID.
PUT /api/users/129/address
Now attacker updates another user’s address.

Why this works?
The system trusts the user ID provided in request.
But it should instead rely on the session identity.
Correct logic:
if request.user_id != session.user_id
   deny

Attack chain:
Attacker account
       │
       ▼
Modify user ID parameter
       │
       ▼
Change victim profile
       │
       ▼
Hijack shipments / personal data

Real consequences:
Attackers could:
change email
reset password
redirect deliveries

Mitigation:
Never trust IDs from the client.
Use: session.user_id
instead of: request.user_id

3. Vertical Privilege Escalation → Admin Access
Vertical escalation means:
Regular user → Admin privileges

Real-world scenario:
A project management SaaS tool.

Architecture:
             Browser
                │
                ▼
        ┌─────────────────┐
        │  Web Application │
        └─────────────────┘
                │
        ┌───────┴────────┐
        ▼                ▼
 User APIs           Admin APIs
        │                │
        ▼                ▼
        Database

Normal Admin Flow:
Admin performs action:
POST /admin/create-user
Server checks:
if user.role != admin
    deny

Vulnerable Implementation:
Developers only check authentication.
if user.logged_in == true
   allow

Attack Flow:
Attacker sends:
POST /admin/create-user
Since authentication exists, the request succeeds.

Result:
Attacker creates an admin account.
Attack chain:
Normal user login
        │
        ▼
Discover hidden admin API
        │
        ▼
Direct API call
        │
        ▼
Admin functionality executed

Why this happens?
Front-end hides admin buttons:
if role == admin
   show admin UI
But the backend does not enforce authorization.
Security must always be enforced server-side.

Mitigation:
Implement server authorization checks.
Example:
if user.role != "admin":
   return 403
Every admin endpoint must enforce this.

4. Mass Assignment → Privilege Escalation
Mass assignment occurs when frameworks automatically bind request parameters to objects.

Real-world scenario:
A user registration API.
Architecture:
Browser
   │
   ▼
Registration API
   │
   ▼
ORM (Object Mapper)
   │
   ▼
Database

Vulnerable Code Example:
user = new User(request.body)
database.save(user)
If request body contains:
{
 "username": "attacker",
 "password": "123",
 "role": "admin"
}
Framework automatically assigns:
user.role = admin

Attack Flow:
Attacker submits:
POST /api/register
Body:
{
 "username":"attacker",
 "password":"123",
 "role":"admin"
}

Database result:
user:
 username = attacker
 role = admin
Attacker now has admin privileges.

Why this happens?
Many frameworks support automatic field mapping.
Examples include:
Spring Boot
Rails
Laravel
Django serializers
Developers forget to restrict sensitive fields.

Mitigation:
Use explicit field allowlists.
Example:
User.create({
 username: request.username,
 password: request.password
})
Never bind entire request bodies automatically.

5. Missing Function-Level Access Control → Hidden Admin API Abuse
This vulnerability occurs when endpoints exist but are not protected.

Real-world architecture:
         Browser
            │
            ▼
       API Gateway
            │
            ▼
        Backend APIs
     ┌─────────┬─────────┐
     ▼         ▼         ▼
User APIs   Admin APIs   Metrics APIs

Normal expectation:
Only admins should access:
GET /api/admin/reports

Vulnerable system:
Endpoint exists but no authorization check.
Attacker simply calls:
GET /api/admin/reports
Server responds.

Attack chain:
Attacker scans API
       │
       ▼
Find hidden endpoint
       │
       ▼
Call endpoint directly
       │
       ▼
Sensitive data exposed

Why this occurs?
Developers rely on:
Frontend restrictions, Instead of backend enforcement.
But attackers can bypass the frontend entirely.

Mitigation:
Enforce role-based access control.
Example middleware:
requireRole("admin")
Every admin endpoint must use it.

Mental Model That Helps You Remember
All these vulnerabilities are authorization failures.
Authorization must happen at three layers.

User Authentication
        │
        ▼
Endpoint Authorization
        │
        ▼
Object-Level Authorization

6. IDOR → Cloud Storage Exposure → Data Exfiltration
This scenario is common in file-sharing SaaS platforms.
Architecture:
                Internet
                    │
                    ▼
              Web Browser
                    │
                    ▼
            Web Application
          (File Management API)
                    │
         ┌──────────┴──────────┐
         ▼                     ▼
     Metadata DB           Object Storage
                          (files, images)
The database stores metadata:
file_id
owner_id
storage_path

Example:
file_id: 84231
owner_id: userA
path: s3://bucket/file84231.pdf

Normal Flow:
User opens their file.
GET /api/files/84231
Server performs:
SELECT * FROM files WHERE file_id = 84231
Then generates a temporary download URL.
https://storage.cloud/files/file84231.pdf

Vulnerability:
The backend does not check owner_id.
It should verify:
file.owner_id == session.user_id
But instead it simply fetches by ID.

Attack Flow:
Attacker changes the file ID.
GET /api/files/84232
Now the API retrieves another user’s file metadata and returns a valid download URL.

Attack Chain:
Attacker account
      │
      ▼
Enumerate file IDs
      │
      ▼
Access metadata for other files
      │
      ▼
Generate valid cloud download URLs
      │
      ▼
Download sensitive documents

Why this happens?
Developers assume that:
If user authenticated → file must belong to them
But object ownership must always be verified.

Mitigation:
Object authorization enforcement:
SELECT * FROM files
WHERE file_id = ?
AND owner_id = current_user
Also generate short-lived storage URLs.

7. Horizontal Privilege Escalation → Payment Fraud
This scenario appears frequently in financial or subscription systems.
Architecture:
 Browser
    │
    ▼
Payment Service API
    │
    ▼
Payment Processor
    │
    ▼
Transaction Database

Transactions are stored as:
transaction_id
user_id
amount
status

Normal Flow:
User requests refund.
POST /api/refund
{
  "transaction_id": 90341
}
Server executes:
UPDATE transactions
SET status = refunded
WHERE transaction_id = 90341

Vulnerability:
The system does not verify:
transaction.user_id == session.user_id

Attack Flow:
Attacker uses someone else’s transaction ID.
POST /api/refund
{
  "transaction_id": 90342
}
The system processes the refund.

Attack Chain:
Attacker logs in
       │
       ▼
Observe transaction ID format
       │
       ▼
Guess another ID
       │
       ▼
Send refund request
       │
       ▼
System refunds victim transaction

Why this happens?
Developers trust transaction ID supplied by client.
But IDs should only be used as lookup keys after authorization checks.

Mitigation:
Authorization validation:
if transaction.user_id != session.user_id
    reject request

8. Mass Assignment → Admin Role Injection
Mass assignment vulnerabilities appear in frameworks with automatic object mapping.
Architecture:
Browser
   │
   ▼
User Profile API
   │
   ▼
ORM Mapper
   │
   ▼
Database

User object fields:
id
username
email
role
account_status

Vulnerable Code:
user.update(request.body)
The framework maps every field automatically.

Normal Update Request:
PATCH /api/profile
{
 "email": "user@mail.com"
}

Attack Request:
Attacker modifies body.
PATCH /api/profile

{
 "email": "attacker@mail.com",
 "role": "admin"
}

What happens internally?
The ORM performs:
user.role = "admin"
Database update:
UPDATE users
SET email='attacker@mail.com',
role='admin'

Attack Chain:
Attacker account
      │
      ▼
Modify request body
      │
      ▼
Inject admin role field
      │
      ▼
ORM automatically updates role
      │
      ▼
Attacker becomes admin

Why this happens?
Frameworks assume all input fields are safe.
But sensitive fields like:
role
permissions
account_status
should never be client-controlled.

Mitigation:
Use explicit field allowlists.
allowed_fields = ["email", "phone"]
Reject all other fields.

