POST /api/deploy
They manually send request.
POST /api/deploy
{
  "service": "payment-api"
}

Attack Chain:
Normal user login
       │
       ▼
Inspect frontend API calls
       │
       ▼
Discover hidden endpoint
       │
       ▼
Call deployment API directly
       │
       ▼
Trigger unauthorized infrastructure changes

Potential impact:
Attacker may:
deploy malicious code
restart services
delete production instances

Why this happens?
Developers assume:
Frontend UI restrictions = security
But attackers can directly call backend APIs.

Mitigation:
Server-side authorization middleware.
Example:
if user.role != "devops"
    return 403




Memory Model:
Authorization must be enforced at three critical levels:
1. Endpoint Level
   Who can call this API?
2. Object Level
   Which resource can they access?
3. Data Binding Level
   What fields can they modify?

Failures create:
IDOR → object access bypass
Horizontal escalation → access another user's data
Vertical escalation → access higher privilege actions
Mass assignment → privilege injection
Missing function control → hidden admin functionality exposed
Missing endpoint checks → vertical escalation
Object ownership failure → IDOR
User identity mismatch → horizontal escalation
Automatic field binding → mass assignment



